A||N||10||bHooper||clutch installation questions.||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||04-29-2000||03:41 PM||||ok, im installing the clutch in the car ( something i have never done b4), and i have a few questions.<P>1) when mounting the pressure plate to the flywheel, what is the proper torque on the bolts?<P>2) should there be any movement on the clutch?  or should it be snug between the flywheel and the pressure plate?<P>all advice is welcome!<P>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-29-2000||04:21 PM||||According to the book the torque on the preasure plate is about 20 ft/lbs to the flywheel. When the preasure plate is on the flywheel that clutch plate BETTER be snug! Like not moving! Like not slipping! This is what the clutch is doing when you take your foot off the clutch pedal. Have fun! <P>PS Make sure that the flywheel bolts are up to 60 and have loctite on them. Can't be too safe!||||reg||2
Z||000002||bHooper||04-29-2000||04:28 PM||||will do... <P>btw, is there anything else that i should be aware of?<P>thanks<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fret Naught||04-29-2000||04:35 PM||||Yes, you are probably aware of this, but when I did my first Clutch I wasn't make sure that you torque the pressure plate equally and evenly, using a torque wrench.||||reg||14
Z||000004||bHooper||04-29-2000||05:56 PM||||thats all done, but now we have a new problem... cant seem to mate the tranny back on to the engine... any tips?<P>thanks<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||04-29-2000||06:07 PM||||Not trying to be a wise guy but you did use an alignment tool? Sometimes you have to put the tranny in gear and turn the wheels a bit to get the spline into the clutch disk||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||04-29-2000||06:19 PM||||If you can get the tranny back out enough to get your head/eye directly in line with the crankshaft centerline and using a small flashlight,make sure the clutch disc is centered,or just go get a small tool made for this if you're not confident with your eyeballs.<BR>The disc actually floats around without the tranny shaft to keep it centered.<BR>Don't force it either,all that should be needed is maybe a slight tug on the flywheel to match up the splines to each other. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||04-29-2000||06:23 PM||||Ok... so maybe i need a proper "education" on tranny installation.  i thought the alignment tool was mostly for "mounting" the clutch and pressure plate to the flywheel... once that had been accomplished, the job was done.  guess i was wrong  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>so what is the corrrect way to go about this, now that i have the clutch and PP mounted to the flywheel?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||04-29-2000||10:47 PM||||Sounds like you got the clutch assembled using the tool ok,I would try again,putting the two together as straight as possible.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroshop||04-30-2000||12:12 AM||||Ok guys I guess I'll let you in on a secret.<BR>I use two long bolts that I have cut the head off and put them in on the top two holes. I them install the starter and put a bat to it and with my remote starter I am able to turn the flywheel with the starter. I then suspend the transmission on the two bolts and as I hit the remote stsrter and the engine starts to rotate I very easily just slide the transmission into place and then bolt it to the engine. I then take the starter out and install the dust cover and all the rest of the gear that has to go on. My back won't take the heavy lift and fighting the trans. Use a little brain and not brawn and safe your back.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||04-30-2000||02:31 AM||||thanks, George... but i'm still confused.. my brain might help... if i had ever done this b4!<P>hoop||||reg||1
